{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 11, :comments ["<b>Observe for yourself.</b> One must know what he is to observe in this, for if he is to observe the matters according to the words of Rabbi Abraham ibn Ezra, there would have been no need to warn him about a particular commandment above all the commandments of the Torah, and it is obvious that he would not suppress what was said to him in prophecy. Perhaps, however, since Israel served idolatry, therefore HaShem contrived to heal their fracture through their opposing the idolaters and hoping to destroy them and distance them so that they not draw near to them — by this their iniquity would be atoned and their repentance would be accepted. And even though they had not yet reached the land, the desire and will in this matter would be effective for atonement for the thing. This is the meaning of His saying <b>Observe for yourself</b> — in the manner of (Genesis 37:11) ״וְאָבִיו שָׁמַר אֶת הַדָּבָר״ — that they should be watching: when will the time come to perform all that is written, to make that matter in which they stumbled come to pass." "<b>Or</b> it may say that previously, before they made the calf, HaShem had not commanded all this distancing from the nations and their abhorrence, and since the evil deed preceded, HaShem added for them this commandment according to the principle of distancing one who stumbles in transgression, who needs to fence himself with a new fence. This is the meaning of His saying <b>Observe for yourself</b> — namely, this commandment has come to you on account of your having made the calf." "<b>Or</b> it is possible that before the deed of the calf, HaShem would not have commanded concerning the matter stated in this passage, for immediately HaShem would have driven out from before them all the inhabitants of the land and there would be no place for the commandment; and there would have been no need to worry about the beasts of the field — either because of the fear of Israel, such that the evil impulse would have ceased from them and the beasts of the field would serve them, or because they would multiply greatly and fill the land. But after the calf the matter was nullified and they needed to be commanded concerning this. This is the meaning of His saying <b>Observe for yourself.</b>" "<b>And by way of</b> allusion it is possible that the word <b>for yourself</b> refers back to Moses, and He hinted to him that what He says to him — <b>Behold, I am driving out from before you</b>, etc. — is not for the immediate time but for a distant time. This accords with their statement, of blessed memory (Numbers Rabbah 19:13): Moses who redeemed us, he will redeem us in the future to come. And concerning that time He says <b>Observe for yourself</b>. And know that the words of HaShem that He said here to Moses, ״Behold, I am driving out from before you,״ etc., were not fulfilled; therefore He said <b>Observe for yourself</b> — for the end of days, that which I command you today."], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 34, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:36:42.772230695Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}